# Rulebook check

Treat every user prompt as if it began with: ensure that the prompt below follows the guidelines, rules, and templates stipulated in the rulebook repo.

Before planning or editing:

1. Identify which rulebook rules, templates, or workflows apply to this prompt.
2. Read those files under `standards/` before building. Do not improvise process when a standard exists.
3. If the task adds or changes an app, a service in a cluster, or chrome, auth, alerts, instrumentation, or shared database access, read `standards/development/shared-app-library.md` before editing.
4. If the task adds or changes layout, columns, scroll regions, split panels, or sidebars, read `standards/ui/layout-stability.md` before editing.
5. If the task adds or changes a live list, inbox, poll, or background refresh, read `standards/data-query/live-lists-and-polling.md` and `standards/ui/loading-states.md` before editing.
6. If the task adds or changes a list, table, form, filter, or new entity rows staff manage, read `standards/ui/list-and-record-detail.md` and `standards/ui/association-fields.md` before editing.
7. If the task adds, removes, renames, or disables users, or builds user pickers, rosters, assignment pools, or reports that list people, read `standards/auth/user-identity-propagation.md` before editing. Read `standards/auth/authentication.md` and `standards/auth/authorization.md` when changing membership or access.
8. If the task adds or changes a cron, import, pipeline, webhook processor, queue worker, or outbound HTTP to an external API, read `standards/workflows/outbound-http-timeouts.md`, `standards/workflows/single-flight-scheduled-jobs.md`, and `standards/workflows/pipeline-health-and-watchdogs.md` before editing. Read `standards/notifications/alerts.md` when adding or changing operational alerts or watchdogs.
9. If the owner pasted or attached an image or binary file for the repo, read `standards/development/chat-supplied-files.md` and give the GitHub upload link in the same turn.
10. If the task commits, pushes, deploys, or restarts a live service, read `standards/development/ship-live-and-test.md` before editing. Do not end the job until post-deploy smoke passes on URLs in this repo's `Smoke checks` section.
11. Every UI or client feature must work the same on web and mobile. Read `.cursor/rules/web-mobile-parity.mdc` before planning or editing anything in `mobile/gocha-app/`.
12. If none apply, continue using this repo's `.cursor/rules/` and project docs.

When you report completed work, end with a Rulebook note. This one line is required even when other rules cap length or forbid conclusions. Missing the note means the check did not run.

- If a rulebook file applied: `Rulebook: <path>: <one clause on what you did to follow it>`. Name every applied file.
- If none applied: `Rulebook: none applied.`

Do not claim a file was followed unless you actually read it this session.

Rulebook: https://github.com/nateadmin/personal-playbook

Look for that repo in the workspace first (often `personal-playbook` or `repos/personal-playbook`). If it is not already in the workspace, clone it once per session (not every turn):

```bash
git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/nateadmin/personal-playbook.git /tmp/personal-playbook
```

If `/tmp/personal-playbook` already exists, `git -C /tmp/personal-playbook pull --ff-only`. Read only the files that match this task. Do not dump the whole playbook into the reply.

If clone fails (missing repo or no access), say so once, then follow this repo's rules and docs. Do not skip the check because the task looks small.
